Vulnerability Title

Improper Authorization Leading to User Identity Manipulation in Sandbox Model Creation

Vulnerability Description

The application allows users to create a sandbox model through the Sandbox module. During the sandbox creation process, the client request includes the username parameter which is sent to the server. It was observed that this parameter is not validated on the server side. By intercepting the request and modifying the username value to another user’s name, the sandbox model can be successfully created under the modified username.

This indicates that the application relies on client-supplied user identity instead of server-side session validation, allowing attackers to manipulate the user identity associated with the sandbox model.

Impact

Attackers can create sandbox models under another user’s identity.

Leads to user impersonation and data integrity issues.

Malicious users may perform actions that appear to be executed by other legitimate users.

This can affect audit logs, accountability, and traceability within the system.

May lead to unauthorized access or manipulation of portfolio analysis data.

Mitigation

The application should not trust client-supplied username parameters.

The server must derive the authenticated user identity directly from the session/token (e.g., session ID, JWT, SSO token).

Implement server-side validation to ensure the username parameter cannot be modified by the client.

Enforce proper access control checks before creating sandbox models.

Log and monitor suspicious identity mismatches between session user and request parameters.

